\section*{Optimal Covering Density of Euclidean Balls}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 092; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that every convex body in dimension at least two admits a single-lattice covering of density at most an absolute multiple of $n\log n$; In manuscript 2 (family 092; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that in every sufficiently large dimension, some symmetric convex body has translative covering density at least an absolute multiple of $n\log n$, ruling out a uniform linear bound.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
An absolute constant $C>0$ is claimed such that for every dimension $n\ge2$ and every convex body $K\subset\mathbb R^n$ there is one full-rank lattice $\Lambda$ with
\[
 K+\Lambda=\mathbb R^n,\qquad |K|/\det\Lambda\le Cn\log n.
\]
The ratio is lattice covering density. Neither central symmetry nor smooth boundary is needed, and the covering uses one lattice rather than a union of independently translated lattices.

\paragraph{Manuscript 2.}
For a convex body $K\subset\mathbb R^n$, define its translative covering density by
\[
 \theta_T(K)=\inf_{\substack{X\subset\mathbb R^n\text{ locally finite}\\K+X=\mathbb R^n}}
 |K|\limsup_{R\to\infty}\frac{|X\cap[-R,R]^n|}{(2R)^n}.
\]
The covering centers are not required to form a lattice. The claimed lower bound is
\[
 \exists c>0\ \exists n_0\ \forall n\ge n_0\ \exists K_n=-K_n:
 \quad\theta_T(K_n)>c n\log n,
\]
with $c,n_0$ absolute and every $K_n$ a full-dimensional convex body.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 092; 2026-09-23), a hierarchy of branching lattice blocks supplies usable anchors. Folded Gaussian sampling and simultaneous shears cover a finite collection of horizontal patterns, after which a final small-hole removal produces an exact covering by one lattice.

For manuscript 2 (family 092; 2026-09-23), a random body is tested against a finite family obtained by rounding periodic covering patterns. Cap-intersection and Poisson-witness estimates make successful low-density coverage too unlikely for any pattern; periodization extends the obstruction to all coverings.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The universal upper bound gives order $n\log n$ for balls as a special case. The matching worst-case lower bound uses constructed bodies rather than Euclidean balls, so the optimal asymptotic ball-covering density is not determined.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 092; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: every convex body in dimension at least two admits a single-lattice covering of density at most an absolute multiple of $n\log n$.

This coverage does not establish a formal proof of the complete problem as stated here.

For manuscript 2 (family 092; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: in every sufficiently large dimension, some symmetric convex body has translative covering density at least an absolute multiple of $n\log n$, ruling out a uniform linear bound.

This coverage does not establish a formal proof of the complete problem as stated here.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{CoveringDensity.lean}, \texttt{SingleLatticeCovering.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.CoveringOrder.optimal\_order}, \texttt{OAI.SingleLatticeCovering.single\_lattice\_covering}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

